Q: Using LIKE operator in Entity Framework Core on BinaryData properties A: <p>You are almost there!</p>
<p>First, you need to write a static method for EF Core. It does not need to have an implementation, it's only used for EF Core translation.</p>
<p>Here, C# 14 extension members is used <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/issues/28419#issuecomment-3765703025" rel="nofollow noreferrer">on the <code>EF</code> namespace</a>.</p>
<p><strong>DbFunctionsExtensions.cs</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

namespace SearchBinaryData;

internal static class DbFunctionsExtensions
{
    extension(EF)
    {
        public static bool Like(BinaryData matchExpression, BinaryData pattern)
            =&gt; throw new InvalidOperationException(&quot;This method is for use with Entity Framework Core only and has no in-memory implementation.&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then you need to register that function to EF Core. In your DbContext, add a new override for the <code>OnModelCreating</code> method and tell it how to translate this <code>Like</code> method into an SQL expression. (This requires an additional <code>using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Query.SqlExpressions</code>)</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
{
    modelBuilder
        .HasDbFunction(((Delegate)DbFunctionsExtensions.Like).Method)
        .HasTranslation(args =&gt; new LikeExpression(args[0], args[1], escapeChar: null, typeMapping: null));
}
</code></pre>
<p>You also need to register a default type mapping for <code>BinaryData</code>. Add this line in your <code>ConfigureConventions</code> method of your dbContext.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>configurationBuilder.DefaultTypeMapping&lt;BinaryData&gt;().HasConversion&lt;BinaryDataConverter&gt;();
</code></pre>
<p>Finally, replace your where clause with the new <code>Like</code> function accepting <code>BinaryData</code> parameters:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>.Where(t =&gt; EF.Like(t.JsonPayload, EF.Parameter(BinaryData.FromString(&quot;%Cédric%&quot;))))
</code></pre>
<p>EF Core will translate this into proper SQL and your query will work. 🥳</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>-- Executed DbCommand (14ms) [Parameters=[@FromString='0x2543C3A96472696325' (Size = 8000)], CommandType='Text', CommandTimeout='30']
SELECT [e].[Id], [e].[JsonPayload]
FROM [Examples] AS [e]
WHERE [e].[JsonPayload] LIKE @FromString
</code></pre>

Q: html-webpack-plugin Entrypoint undefined = index.html A: <p>This is a meaningless error in webpack 4, runing with an earlier version of webpack like 3.0.7 or ignoring the error with  <code>stats: {
        children: false,
    }</code> 
will fix this <a href="https://github.com/jantimon/html-webpack-plugin/issues/895" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/jantimon/html-webpack-plugin/issues/895</a></p>

Q: Canvas: Zooming and Panning A: <p>If you're okay with buttons instead of &quot;drag to pan&quot;, you can use <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CanvasRenderingContext2D/translate" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>translate</code></a> method for panning. You can also use <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Canvas_API/Tutorial/Transformations#Translating" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>save()</code> and <code>restore()</code></a> methods or following approach. Here's what I used in similar situation, but I've used buttons:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
window.onload = function() {
   scale = 1;
   canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;); //Replace with id of your canvas
   ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);
   ctx.translate(150, 75);
   drawcanvas();
} 

function drawcanvas() {
  ctx.beginPath();
  //call to function(s) which draws necessary things
  //..
  //..
  drawarrow(ctx);
  ctx.stroke(); 
}

function clearcanvas() {
  let current_transform = ctx.getTransform();
  ctx.setTransform(1.1, 0, 0, 1.1, 0, 0);
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width*scale, canvas.height*scale);
  ctx.setTransform(current_transform);
}

function zoomin() {
  clearcanvas();
  scale = 1.1;
  ctx.scale(scale, scale);
  drawcanvas();
}

function zoomout() {
  clearcanvas();
  scale = 1.0/1.1;
  ctx.scale(scale, scale);
  drawcanvas();
}

function moveleft() {
  clearcanvas();
  ctx.translate(-10, 0);
  drawcanvas();
}

function moveright() {
  clearcanvas();
  ctx.translate(10, 0);
  drawcanvas();
}
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;canvas id=&quot;c&quot; class=&quot;container h-75&quot;  style=&quot;border: solid 1px blue&quot;&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
  &lt;button onclick=&quot;zoomin()&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button onclick=&quot;zoomout()&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button onclick=&quot;moveleft()&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;&lt;-&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button onclick=&quot;moveright()&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;-&gt;&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;


</code></pre>
<p><strong>EDIT:</strong> I found better solution for panning problem, check <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/38142308/canvas-drag-on-mouse-movement">this question</a> and it's accepted answer.</p>

Q: Does ncurses support TrueColor? A: <p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/63611791/11465149">Thomas already answered this</a>. My goal here is to simply provide a clean &amp; minimal-example, focused on modern-terminals. If anyone wants anything more than that, eg. direct + 256 + 16 color support too -- I recommend you to look into <a href="https://github.com/ThomasDickey/ncurses-snapshots/blob/master/test/picsmap.c" rel="nofollow noreferrer" title="specifically look for `map_color` function.">picsmap.c</a>, too.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;ncurses.h&gt;


int main(void) {
    SCREEN *screen;
    int ch;

    // Initialize screen with TERM=xterm-direct
    // (That's your best bet since most people don't use direct)
    screen = newterm(&quot;xterm-direct&quot;, stdout, stdin);

    // Check whether or not was initialized
    if (!screen) {
        fprintf(stderr, &quot;Failed to initialize terminal.\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    // Set screen as active
    set_term(screen);

    // Start colors :P
    start_color();

    // assign terminal-default fore/back-ground to color number -1
    // (-1 now works with transparent windows too.)
    use_default_colors();

    // Initialize color-pairs with direct-colors
    // (No need of init_color or init_extended_color)
    init_extended_pair(3, 0xff12f8, 0x2d1d3d);
    init_extended_pair(2, 0x7aaa00, -1); // default terminal background 
    init_extended_pair(1, 0xff5f00, 0x7d1d3d);

    // Draw/Print some stuff
    color_set(2, NULL);
    printw(&quot;Hello direct-colors!\n&quot;);
    attr_set(A_BOLD | A_ITALIC, 3, NULL);
    printw(&quot;Hello direct-colors!\n&quot;);
    attr_set(A_BOLD | A_REVERSE, 3, NULL);
    printw(&quot;Hello direct-colors!\n&quot;);
    attr_set(A_ITALIC, 1, NULL);
    printw(&quot;Hello direct-colors!\n&quot;);
    attr_set(A_ITALIC | A_REVERSE, 1, NULL);
    printw(&quot;Hello direct-colors!\n&quot;);
    attr_set(A_NORMAL, 0, NULL);
    printw(&quot;Hello direct-colors!\n&quot;);


    // display + wait until key + end stuff
    refresh();
    getch();
    endwin();
    delscreen(screen);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Expected output:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/pB5CDZgf.jpg" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/pB5CDZgf.jpg" alt="expected output" /></a></p>
<p>Now, why do i post this? because I slightly empathised with <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63570631/does-ncurses-support-truecolor#comment140819706_63611791">this</a> + LLMs still hallucinate about this specific part of Ncurses + there wasn't alot of examples out there + because I went so far deep into Ncurses <code>lib_color.c</code> to the point where I thought I had solved the direct-color compatibility with <code>init_color()</code> via something like this, lol:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>if (sp-&gt;_direct_color.value &amp;&amp; b &lt; 256 &amp;&amp; sp-&gt;_color_table[b].init == 1)
      b = (
          ((sp-&gt;_color_table[b].r * 255 / 1000) &lt;&lt; 16) |
          ((sp-&gt;_color_table[b].g * 255 / 1000) &lt;&lt; 8)  |
          (sp-&gt;_color_table[b].b * 255 / 1000)
      );
</code></pre>
<p><em>(so, don't do the same mistake as I did :P)</em></p>
